    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1814</span><span class="rf-text">The defeat at Rancagua ends the Patria Vieja, and Chilean exiles place themselves under San Martín in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyo">Cuyo</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1817</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> sets out from Mendoza to cross the Andes, losing as much as one third of its men and more than half of its horses</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1817</span><span class="rf-text">The royalist conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> adopts Maroto's plan to retreat south on 8 February, and the governor reverses it the next morning</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1817</span><span class="rf-text">The patriot columns meet at the camp of Curimón on 9 February and resolve to attack at dawn on the 12th</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1817</span><span class="rf-text">The two patriot divisions come down on the royalists at Chacabuco on 12 February, and the fighting ends in the afternoon</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1817</span><span class="rf-text">The patriots enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, on 13 February by one account and 14 February by another</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1817</span><span class="rf-text">San Martín declines the governorship and an assembly names O'Higgins Supreme Director</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1818</span><span class="rf-text">The expulsion of Spanish forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> is completed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maipu">Battle of Maipú</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-chacabuco" data-v="5fe4907cf3" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>Two divisions to attack from west and east</b> The patriot troops were split in two for a pincer. Soler's division was to attack from the west and O'Higgins's from the east. The royalists stood in front of the hill of Victoria, near the Chacabuco ranch.</li><li><b>O'Higgins faces the royalist main body</b> O'Higgins came by the shorter, steeper road and met the royalist advance parties, then found himself face to face with the main body. He moved on to the hill Los Halcones and deployed his troops there.</li><li><b>O'Higgins charges the Talavera battalion</b> Without a reply to his messages, O'Higgins ordered his infantry to charge with the bayonet in two columns. They went at the royalist right wing, the Talavera battalion, with Zapiola's cavalry in support.</li><li><b>The charge stops at Las Margaritas</b> The grenadiers met the deep bed of Las Margaritas, which they had not seen, and could not cross in attack formation. After a volley they fell back to the hill Los Halcones and re-formed.</li><li><b>Soler hurries to flank the enemy</b> Other accounts say O'Higgins's charge was thrown back with heavy losses, so that Soler had to hurry to flank the enemy if the battle was not to be lost. His division moved towards the royalist left.</li><li><b>A second assault on the flank and the centre</b> O'Higgins and his adviser sent the troops in again. This time the cavalry went against the royalist right flank and the infantry against the centre.</li><li><b>A cavalry platoon breaks the royalist line</b> A platoon of cavalry broke the royalist line between the far left of the Talavera battalion and the right of the main body of the Chiloé battalion. The infantry, almost victorious, came up to help the cavalry.</li><li><b>Zapiola breaks the Talavera square and passes the wing</b> Zapiola broke the square formed by the Talavera men and went beyond the royalist right wing. A second charge on the royalist infantry and cavalry then scattered them.</li><li><b>The royalists flee towards the houses of Chacabuco</b> The remains of the royalist army fled in disorder towards the houses of Chacabuco, a few kilometres away, leaving a third of their strength on the field. The battle ended at two in the afternoon.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin.jpg" alt="José de San Martín" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_de_San_Mart%C3%ADn_(retrato,_c.1828).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The exact author is disputed. Some sources attribute it to Jean Baptiste Madou, others to the art teacher of San Martin's …, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the defeat at Rancagua in 1814, which ended the period of Chilean history called the Patria Vieja, Chilean exiles went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyo">Cuyo</a> and placed themselves under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a>, governor of the province. San Martín planned to defeat the royalists by attacking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> by sea from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, and the royalist occupation of Chile obliged him to free that country first. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins">Bernardo O'Higgins</a> and Ramón Freire helped to organise and train the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a>. San Martín freed black slaves on condition that they enlisted, and took into the army the Chilean soldiers who followed O'Higgins and those of Carrera willing to serve under his flags.</p><p>In January 1817 the army set out across the Andes. San Martín's planning misled the royalist forces in Chile into defending against threats that did not exist, and the crossing went unchallenged. It was costly all the same: the army lost as much as one third of its men and more than half of its horses. The columns that crossed by the Los Patos road and by the Uspallata road met at the camp of Curimón on 9 February.</p><p>The royalists found it hard to gather an army because their forces were scattered. At a military conference on 8 February, called by the Royal Governor, Field Marshal Casimiro Marcó del Pont, the plan of Brigadier Rafael Maroto was adopted: to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> and retreat south, where the royalists could regroup and gather resources. The following morning the governor reversed the decision and ordered Maroto to prepare for battle at Chacabuco.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes.jpg" alt="Army of the Andes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ejercito_de_los_Andes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Bouchet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Estimates of the size of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> run from as low as 3,500 to as high as 6,000 men. One account of the build-up speaks of approximately 6,000 men, 1,200 horses and 22 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>; another of about 4,000 Chileans and Argentines, well armed and disciplined. About 1,200 auxiliaries handled supply. Black soldiers, mostly freed slaves, are estimated at between 2,000 and 3,000, and Lynch puts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> recruited at 1,554.</p><p>For the battle San Martín divided his troops in two, to attack the royalists from front and rear. The right wing, under Miguel Estanislao Soler, was to attack from the west with the 1st Cazadores and 11th battalions, companies of the 7th and 8th, a squadron of Grenadiers, the commander-in-chief's escort squadron and seven guns: 2,000 men in all. The left wing, under O'Higgins, was to attack from the east with fusilier companies of the 7th and 8th, three squadrons of Horse Grenadiers and two guns: 1,500 men. Soler took the Montenegro road, gentler but much longer; O'Higgins took the Cuesta Vieja, shorter but steep and far more dangerous. Another account has San Martín divide 2,000 troops in all.</p><p>The royalists were fewer: the Talavera battalion of peninsular soldiers and two others drawn mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiloe">Chiloé</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdivia">Valdivia</a>, about 1,500 men, though another account has Maroto leading up to 2,000 volunteers. Their morale was low, as they were badly paid and had not had their ranks from the earlier reconquest recognised. A spy disguised as a roto, a poor Chilean peasant, kept San Martín informed of Marcó's orders and Maroto's plans.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>San Martín sent his troops down the mountain from midnight on 11 February to attack at dawn. Soler's column took longer than expected. O'Higgins met the royalist advance parties and then their main body, and deployed on the hill Los Halcones, sending word to San Martín. Maroto, finding the slope held by the patriots on the 12th, chose to defend the ground near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">hacienda</a> rather than fall back to Colina.</p><p>Accounts of the next hours differ. In one, O'Higgins, receiving no orders, began the attack; San Martín sent word to Soler and went forward himself with O'Higgins until a division of Soler's advance arrived and enveloped the royalists' left flank and rear. In another, O'Higgins, supposedly overcome by the sight of his homeland, abandoned the plan and charged with 1,500 men; he claimed the royalists had stopped retreating and were advancing, and that retreating up the narrow path would have meant being picked off one by one. San Martín ordered Soler to charge the flank.</p><p>A third version says O'Higgins's charge was repulsed with many casualties. At 11:45, against orders not to commit fire and on the advice of the former Napoleonic officer Crámer, he sent two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a> columns against the Talavera battalion on the royalist right, with Zapiola's cavalry. The grenadiers were stopped by the channel of Las Margaritas and fell back to Los Halcones before charging again. Zapiola broke the square of the Talaveras and the royalists fled towards the houses of Chacabuco, leaving a third of their strength. In the other accounts the royalists form a square round the ranch and the fighting is hand to hand there.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Royalist dead are given as 500 and prisoners as 600, one account specifying 32 officers and 600 soldiers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> lost twelve men in the battle; one account adds 120 who died of wounds, another counts 120 wounded. The captured goods included the flag of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiloe">Chiloé</a> regiment, about a thousand rifles and two guns. Maroto escaped, slightly wounded, thanks to the speed of his horse.</p><p>The patriots entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, on 13 February by one account and 14 February by another. Several historians think San Martín erred in not pursuing the beaten royalists, who were able to re-embark 1,600 soldiers for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, the base of Mariano Osorio's expedition; otherwise, they estimate, the expedition to Peru would not have been delayed three years. Another account has the remaining royalists retreat to the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, form a small enclave, be reinforced by sea and in the end withdraw by sea to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>.</p><p>An assembly under the interim governor Francisco Ruiz-Tagle first named San Martín governor of Chile. He refused and called a new assembly of 210 notable residents, and the army's auditor Bernardo de Vera y Pintado repeated his refusal; O'Higgins was acclaimed Supreme Director and held the post until 1823. His first act of government, on 17 February, was a proclamation. News of the victory reached Mendoza on 16 February and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> on 24 February.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>San Martín sent an official report to Juan Martín de Pueyrredón, Supreme Director of the United Provinces, soon after the battle, and the figures for the dead and the prisoners are given in that connection. The fighting itself is told in more than one way. O'Higgins gave his own reasons for leaving the plan, and what took place between his advance and Soler's arrival is fiercely debated. One version has Soler's advance division arrive and complete the envelopment; another has O'Higgins's charge repulsed, with Soler hurrying to save the day, and credits the advice of the Napoleonic officer Crámer.</p><p>The figures are disputed as well. Estimates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> run from 3,500 to 6,000 men, the crossing is dated from 17 or 19 January and given 21 or 25 days, and the entry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> falls on 13 or 14 February. It is not agreed whether 120 patriots were wounded or died of their wounds, nor how many royalists got away: Maroto escaped, and 1,600 soldiers are said to have embarked for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maipu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-maipu.jpg" alt="Battle of Maipú" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maipu">Battle of Maipú</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BatallaDeMaipu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johann Moritz Rugendas, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The victory began the expulsion of Spanish forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, completed the following year at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maipu">Battle of Maipú</a>. It ended the period of the Reconquista, or Restoration, and began the Patria Nueva, with O'Higgins in office until 1823. The Chilean army was reconstituted under Argentine officers, and San Martín was appointed commander of the United Army, which joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> with Chilean troops and fought Osorio's royalist army at Cancha Rayada and Maipú.</p><p>The battle raised the morale of the Army of the Andes and is placed within the wider movement of liberation from Spanish rule across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, in which San Martín was one of the leaders. He refused the governorship in accordance with his own resolve and instructions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, and the Chilean state that followed was led by O'Higgins, who had fought in the left wing at Chacabuco.</p><p>Several ships of the Chilean Navy have been named after the battle, and a Monument to the Victory of Chacabuco commemorates it. The flag taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiloe">Chiloé</a> regiment was carried to Buenos Aires with San Martín's report by Major Manuel Escalada on 26 February.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Chacabuco on 12 February 1817 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a> under San Martín defeated a royalist force of about 1,500 under Maroto, and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> within days. Accounts of the fighting differ; O'Higgins became Supreme Director of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>.</em></p>

